# Define acronyms at first use

Scientific prose must define every acronym, even familiar ones. Do not assume the reader knows VR, TDA, CUSUM, or similar.

At the first occurrence in a deliverable, write the full form, then the acronym in parentheses. After that, the short form is allowed.

```latex
% BAD
the VR complex $\mathrm{VR}(P(t))$

% GOOD
the Vietoris--Rips (VR) complex $\mathrm{VR}(P(t))$
```

```markdown
BAD: CUSUM on the landscape series
GOOD: cumulative sum (CUSUM) on the landscape series
```

## Scope

- Applies to papers, grants, captions, abstracts, and collaborator notes.
- Homology degrees ($H_0$, $H_1$) and standard mathematical operators are notation, not acronyms.
- Do not redefine the same acronym in every section of one paper.
- If the short form never appears, do not invent an acronym.
- Journal keywords may keep the full name only.